{#- Three recommendations, one format: problem, action, sized impact (in revenue
    over the window), metric with target, confidence, main risk. Revenue ranges
    use revenue per funnel purchase; the low end halves the conversion of the
    added traffic. Targets marked as assumptions are planning choices. -#}
  <div class="rec-grid">

    <div class="rec">
      <span class="rec-priority p1">Priority 1</span>
      <h4>Fix discovery on the weakest landing pages</h4>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Problem</div>
        <p><strong>{{ total_lost_discovery|fmt(',') }} sessions ({{ (100 - view_rate)|fmt('.0f') }}%)</strong> never see a product.
        {% if weak_pages_n %}{{ weak_pages_n }} landing page{{ 's' if weak_pages_n > 1 }} take{{ '' if weak_pages_n > 1 else 's' }} {{ weak_landings|fmt(',') }} landings
        ({{ weak_share|fmt('.0f') }}% of sessions) but show a product to far fewer of them than the site's
        {{ view_rate|fmt('.1f') }}%. Comparing the visitors who land on each page with those who reach it from
        inside the site, week by week, separates the causes:{% endif %}</p>
        {% if weak_pages_n %}
        <table class="dev-table rec-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Landing page · diagnosis</th><th>Landings</th><th>Saw a product</th><th>From inside</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
{% for r in weak_page_rows -%}
<tr><td class="path">{{ r.landing_page }}<span class="diag diag-{{ r.diagnosis|replace(' ', '-') }}">{{ r.diagnosis }}</span></td><td>{{ r.sessions|int|fmt(',') }}</td><td class="dev-key">{{ r.view_pct|fmt('.1f') }}%</td><td>{{ r.inside_rate|fmt('.1f') }}%</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">Saw a product: landing sessions that saw any product (site: {{ view_rate|fmt('.1f') }}%).
        From inside: visitors who reached the page from another page and saw products on it.</p>
        {% if entry_pages_n %}<p><strong>Entry traffic{% if entry_pages_n > 1 %} ({{ entry_pages_n }} pages){% endif %}.</strong> The page{{ 's work' if entry_pages_n > 1 else ' works' }}:
        visitors who reach {{ 'them' if entry_pages_n > 1 else 'it' }} from inside the site see products
        {% if entry_inside_lo|round(1) != entry_inside_hi|round(1) %}{{ entry_inside_lo|fmt('.1f') }}–{{ entry_inside_hi|fmt('.1f') }}%{% else %}{{ entry_inside_lo|fmt('.1f') }}%{% endif %} of the time
        and stay {{ entry_inside_eng|fmt('.0f') }}s on average; visitors who land there stay {{ entry_landed_eng|fmt('.0f') }}s,
        and {{ entry_untagged_pct|fmt('.0f') }}% of those landings carry no channel tag.</p>{% endif %}
        {% for r in tracking_pages %}<p><strong>Tracking</strong> (<code>{{ r.landing_page }}</code>). <code>view_item</code> was
        almost silent from {{ r.gap_from }} to {{ r.gap_to }} ({{ r.gap.rate_in|fmt('.1f') }}% of visits, against
        {{ r.gap.rate_out|fmt('.1f') }}% in other weeks) while visitors scrolled much as usual ({{ r.gap.scroll_in|fmt('.1f') }}%
        against {{ r.gap.scroll_out|fmt('.1f') }}%); it fires again from {{ r.resumed }}. A measurement gap, not lost
        product views, so it is left out of the sizing.</p>{% endfor %}
        {% if page_fail_n %}<p><strong>Page{% if page_fail_n > 1 %} ({{ page_fail_n }}){% endif %}.</strong> Visitors see few products
        whether they land or arrive from inside the site: the page itself does not show them.</p>{% endif %}
        {% endif %}
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Action</div>
        <p>{% if entry_pages_n %}For the entry-traffic page{{ 's' if entry_pages_n > 1 }}: find what sends the landing
        visitors — most carry no channel tag, so start with campaign, partner and email links without UTM
        parameters, then organic results whose snippet does not match the page — and send that traffic to
        pages that hold visitors, or show products above the fold to first-time visitors.{% endif %}
        {% if page_fail_n %}For the failing page{{ 's' if page_fail_n > 1 }}: open {{ 'them' if page_fail_n > 1 else 'it' }} as a
        first-time visitor and check that the product list loads and the products are in stock.{% endif %}
        {% if tracking_pages %}For tracking: add an alert when a page's <code>view_item</code> rate drops to near zero for a
        week, so a silent tag is caught in days, not weeks.{% endif %}
        {% if not weak_pages_n %}No landing page with real volume sits far below the site rate, so the loss is
        spread across pages: test product lists above the fold on the home page.{% endif %}
        {%- if home_share %} The home page takes {{ home_share|fmt('.0f') }}% of landings and shows a product to
        {{ home_view_pct|fmt('.1f') }}% of them; test a product grid above the fold there next.{% endif %}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section rec-impact">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Sized impact</div>
        {% if real_pages_n %}
        <p>Bringing the {{ real_pages_n }} page{{ 's' if real_pages_n > 1 }} {{ 'that are' if real_pages_n > 1 else 'that is' }} not a tracking gap to the site rate would add
        ~{{ weak_extra_views|fmt(',') }} product-viewing sessions, ~{{ rec1_orders|fmt(',') }} orders at today's
        {{ (view_to_purchase_rate * 100)|fmt('.1f') }}% saw → purchase rate:
        <strong>${{ rec1_rev_low|fmt(',.0f') }}–${{ rec1_rev_high|fmt(',.0f') }}</strong> over the window
        (low end: the added sessions convert at half today's rate).</p>
        {% elif weak_pages_n %}
        <p>Every weak page is a tracking gap, so there is no product-view opportunity to size.</p>
        {% else %}
        <p>See the +5pp scenario in the funnel section: <strong>${{ lift_5pp_rev_50|fmt(',.0f') }}–${{ lift_5pp_revenue|fmt(',.0f') }}</strong>.</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Metric · target</div>
        <p>Share of {{ "these pages' landings" if real_pages_n > 1 else "the page's landings" }} that see a product · {{ view_rate|fmt('.1f') }}% (the site rate)</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Confidence</div>
        <p><strong>Medium.</strong> The diagnosis is measured — landing and inside visitors, week by week, against
        stated thresholds (see the appendix). What sends the low-intent landing traffic is not yet known.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Main risk</div>
        <p>{% if entry_pages_n %}Many of these landings are very short, untagged sessions; if a large share are
        automated or accidental visits, the reachable opportunity is smaller than sized.{% endif %}
        {% if channel_is_driver %}Channel reach differs by {{ channel_view_spread|fmt('.1f') }}pp, so part of the gap may be the traffic, not the page.{% endif %}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec">
      <span class="rec-priority p2">Priority 2</span>
      <h4>Recover carts and bring visitors back</h4>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Problem</div>
        <p><strong>{{ cart_abandon_pct|fmt('.0f') }}% of carting sessions</strong> ({{ abandoned_n|fmt(',') }} of
        {{ carted_n|fmt(',') }}) never begin checkout, and Week-1 return is <strong>{{ wk1_display }}</strong>.
        {% if has_visitor_split %}Returning sessions buy at {{ visitor_cvr_ratio|fmt('.1f') }}× the rate of new ones.{% endif %}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Action</div>
        <p>For identifiable users, send a cart reminder <strong>within 24h</strong>, browsed products on
        <strong>day 3</strong> and a win-back on <strong>day 7</strong>. Test against a holdout group.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section rec-impact">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Sized impact</div>
        <p>Cart recovery: if {{ (CART_RECOVERY * 100)|fmt('.0f') }}% of abandoned carts reach checkout (an assumption) and
        complete at today's {{ checkout_to_purch|fmt('.0f') }}%, ~{{ rec2_cart_orders|fmt(',') }} orders,
        <strong>~${{ rec2_cart_rev|fmt(',.0f') }}</strong>.
        {% if has_visitor_split %}Re-engagement: Week-1 return at {{ WK1_TARGET|fmt('.0f') }}% brings back ~{{ wk1_extra_users|fmt(',') }} users;
        one session each, valued between a new and a returning session,
        <strong>${{ rec2_rev_low|fmt(',.0f') }}–${{ rec2_rev_high|fmt(',.0f') }}</strong>.{% endif %}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Metric · target</div>
        <p>Cart → checkout {{ cart_to_checkout|fmt('.1f') }}% → {{ rec2_checkout_target|fmt('.1f') }}% · Week-1 return
        {{ wk1_display }} → {{ WK1_TARGET|fmt('.0f') }}% (both planning assumptions)</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Confidence</div>
        <p><strong>Low to medium.</strong> The gap between new and returning sessions is measured, but
        users who return unprompted were keener to begin with, and this data cannot show whether any
        re-engagement already runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Main risk</div>
        <p>Reach: only signed-in or subscribed users can be emailed, and most sessions here are
        anonymous. Re-engaged users will convert below today's returning rate.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec">
      <span class="rec-priority p3">Priority 3</span>
      <h4>Audit the weakest product listings</h4>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Problem</div>
        <p>{% if category_top_targets %}{{ category_n_below }} of {{ n_categories }} product-type listing pages turn
        sightings into carts below the {{ category_benchmark|fmt('.1f') }}% product-type average, with the whole 95%
        interval below it. The most carts are at stake on {% for c in category_top_targets %}<strong>{{ c }}</strong>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.{% else %}No product-type listing sits
        clearly below the {{ category_benchmark|fmt('.1f') }}% average: the seen → carted loss is spread across the catalogue.{% endif %}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Action</div>
        <p>{% if category_top_targets %}Review how {{ category_top_targets|join(', ') }} products are shown in listings: images,
        price display, sizes and stock, and whether quick-add works. Change one page at a time against the
        others as a control.{% else %}Look at price and stock across the catalogue rather than at single pages.{% endif %}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section rec-impact">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Sized impact</div>
        <p>{% include "partials/rec3_impact.html.j2" %}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Metric · target</div>
        <p>Seen → carted on the audited pages · {{ category_benchmark|fmt('.1f') }}% (the product-type average)</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Confidence</div>
        <p><strong>Medium.</strong> Flags need the whole interval below average, but with {{ n_categories }} pages tested about
        {{ (n_categories * 0.05)|fmt('.0f') }} flag(s) could be chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-section">
        <div class="rec-section-label">Main risk</div>
        <p>Price, not presentation. Items at {{ price_best_band }} cart at {{ price_best_pct|fmt('.1f') }}% and items at
        {{ price_worst_band }} at {{ price_worst_pct|fmt('.1f') }}%{% if flagged_median_price is not none and other_median_price is not none %};
        the flagged pages' products have a median price of ${{ flagged_median_price|fmt('.0f') }} against
        ${{ other_median_price|fmt('.0f') }} for the rest{% endif %}. Part of each gap may be price.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
